window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][28] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you run a sensitivity check on a ranked list? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I change the most uncertain inputs and see whether the order moves. If the top item's score depends on a reach estimate I am unsure of, I vary that estimate by a third in each direction and re-rank; if the top two swap, the choice is not robust and I would test the assumption before committing. I also check how much better the first item is than the second: a 5% gap is a coin flip, a 40% gap is a decision. The point is to learn which assumption decides the ranking and to put effort there, not to defend a number.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you bring stakeholders along? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By sharing the goal, the criteria and the capacity before the ranking, so that the result is the consequence of rules they accepted. I show the ranked list with the cut line at capacity, what is below the line and why, and what would change the order. I ask each stakeholder for the evidence behind their item and say what I would need to move it up. People accept a no more easily when they see the tradeoff and have a path to a yes, and a named owner makes the final call.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c15-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C15-02 · Beginner · execution · EX</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> In a scoping meeting, every stakeholder says their items are &quot;must-haves.&quot; How do you handle it?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd get everyone to agree&quot; or &quot;I'd decide myself.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses a tool such as MoSCoW with a constraint to force tradeoffs.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Anchors &quot;must&quot; to the goal and the date, uses capacity to test the list, makes tradeoffs visible, and leaves the decision with a named owner.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> When everything is a must, the word has lost its meaning, so I'd redefine it against a constraint. A &quot;must&quot; is something without which the release fails its goal or cannot ship at the date (a legal requirement, a core flow). I'd restate the goal and the date, then ask of each item: &quot;If this were missing at launch, would the release still deliver its goal?&quot;</p>\n<p>Then I'd bring capacity into the room. Suppose the &quot;musts&quot; total 40 person-weeks and we have 24 with a buffer. I'd say: &quot;These can't all be musts; we have capacity for 24. What leaves?&quot; Putting the number in front of people shifts the conversation from defending items to choosing among them. I'd use MoSCoW (must, should, could, won't this time) to sort, with the &quot;won't this time&quot; column given as much attention as &quot;must,&quot; because it shows people the item is deferred and not dismissed, with a date.</p>\n<p>For disputes I'd offer options with costs rather than argue: &quot;If we keep A, we lose C and D; if we keep C and D, A moves to the next release. Which serves the goal?&quot; The decision belongs to a named owner, usually me with the lead engineer and designer, with the sponsor informed. After the meeting, I'd send the written list with the reasons, so the criteria are on record. What would change my mind: new evidence that a deferred item is a blocker for the goal.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you use capacity to test the must-have list? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By adding it up. If the team has 40 person-weeks and the musts total 70, the list is not a list of musts, because at least 30 weeks of it cannot be built. I would put the capacity number on the screen and ask the group to cut or defer until the total fits, starting with items whose absence would not break the goal or the date. That turns an argument about importance into a visible arithmetic problem. Items that stay are the true musts, and the rest become a ranked next list with reasons.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Who decides when stakeholders still disagree? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A named owner, agreed before the meeting. In most teams the PM proposes and the PM's manager or a product leader decides when there is a cross-team conflict. I would write the options, the tradeoffs and my recommendation in a one-page note, ask the decider for a decision by a date, and record it. Everyone gets heard and the decision is made once. I would also say what evidence would reopen the decision, so that it is not reopened by volume.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c15-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C15-03 · Intermediate · execution · EX · Apple</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Three candidates for the next system release: better notifications, a new widget, a battery mode. Pick one.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The battery mode, because people care about battery.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States a goal, derives criteria, compares the three on them, and picks one with a reason.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows the ranking depends on the goal, runs a sensitivity check, names tradeoffs (including effects on developers, privacy and other devices), tests the riskiest assumption, and says what would change the choice.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I know the product only from public information; nothing here describes any real plan. I'd first ask what the release is meant to achieve, because the right pick depends on it. Assume the goal is to raise day-to-day satisfaction for the largest group of users across a range of devices, with a secondary aim of keeping developers engaged. I'd say this is my assumption and ask whether it is right.</p>\n<p><em>Criteria from the goal.</em> How many users benefit, how much it improves their day (impact), how sure we are, how much it costs to build, and what it does to others: developers (a widget depends on them), the user's other devices, privacy, and the platform's consistency.</p>\n<p><em>Sizing (invented).</em> Reach as a share of users, impact on a 0.25 to 3 scale, confidence, and effort in person-months.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Reach</th>\n<th>Impact</th>\n<th>Confidence</th>\n<th>Effort</th>\n<th>Score</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Battery mode</td>\n<td>60%</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>70%</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>21.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Better notifications</td>\n<td>80%</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>50%</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>5.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>New widget</td>\n<td>40%</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>60%</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>4.8</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Reasoning behind the numbers.</em> Battery mode reaches the people who run low in a day (most mobile users sometimes), has a measurable benefit (hours of use) and is a setting, which is lower risk to existing behavior. Notifications touch nearly everyone, but a better version is hard to define, so confidence is low, effort is high, and changes affect developers and the experience of users who like theirs (the legacy-user question). A widget depends on developers adopting it, so its reach is limited by their uptake.</p>\n<p><em>Sensitivity.</em> The battery mode's impact is the key number. If it is 1 instead of 2, its score is 10.5, still more than twice the others. For the notifications to win, their confidence and effort would both have to be far better than assumed. So the choice is robust to this uncertainty. It would change if the goal changed: if the aim were to strengthen the developer ecosystem, the widget would rise, since it creates something for developers to build.</p>\n<p><em>Riskiest assumption and test.</em> That users would turn on a battery mode and accept its tradeoffs (reduced performance or background activity). A cheap test is a prototype setting with a small group measuring opt-in and satisfaction, with a guardrail on complaints about slowness. I'd also check the interaction with other devices (does it sync across a user's devices?).</p>\n<p><em>Decision.</em> &quot;I'd pick the battery mode: it serves the most users with the clearest benefit for the least cost and risk. I'd defer notifications until we can define the improvement, and the widget until developer demand is clearer. What would change my mind: evidence that the benefit is under half what I assumed, or a goal that favors the ecosystem.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that prioritization prompts at Apple involve choosing among candidate features for a release <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any real release, plan or user research.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How does the ranking change if the goal changes? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Each goal rewards different items. If the goal is daily satisfaction for the largest group, better notifications win on reach; if the goal is battery confidence for users of older devices, the battery mode wins on severity for that segment; if the goal is developer engagement, the widget may win because it gives developers a new surface. That is why I ask for the goal first and state my assumption. I would show the interviewer the three rankings in one table so that they can see how the choice depends on the goal.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you test the riskiest assumption for your pick? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Find the assumption that would make the pick wrong, usually that the improvement is noticed. For notifications I would test whether users change behavior after a redesign using a staged release with a holdout, measuring the share of notifications acted on and the share silenced. Privacy constraints shape the test: use on-device measures and aggregated counts. If the redesign increases silencing, it fails, and I would switch to the next item. A two-week test in a beta group would decide before the full commitment.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c15-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C15-04 · Intermediate · execution · EX · Microsoft</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Three large enterprise customers each want a different thing: admin controls, better performance, more integrations. They are 12%, 9% and 6% of revenue. How do you prioritize?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Do the biggest customer's request first.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Separates revenue at risk from breadth of need and effort, and looks beyond the three.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes blockers (compliance) from preferences, finds shared needs, sizes with a framework and sensitivity, protects against concentration and precedent, and plans communication.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd avoid ranking by customer size, because it makes the roadmap a function of who shouts loudest and ignores the other 197 customers. My approach: separate what each request is <em>for</em>, how broad the need is, and what it costs.</p>\n<p><em>Understand the needs.</em> For each customer, ask what outcome they want and what happens if they do not get it. The largest customer's admin controls might be a compliance requirement (a blocker, with a deadline) or a convenience. If it is a compliance blocker, it is a constraint: either we meet it or we risk the account; I'd then look for the smallest set of controls that satisfies the requirement.</p>\n<p><em>Breadth.</em> Check the wider base. Suppose (invented) of 200 customers, 30 have asked for admin controls, 55 for performance and 80 for integrations.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Customers (reach)</th>\n<th>Impact</th>\n<th>Confidence</th>\n<th>Effort (pm)</th>\n<th>Score</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Integrations</td>\n<td>80</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>70%</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>14.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Performance</td>\n<td>55</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>60%</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>7.3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Admin controls</td>\n<td>30</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>80%</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>4.0</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Integrations score highest because of breadth and low effort. Performance has the highest impact per user but costs the most. Admin controls reach the fewest customers, but that count is not the whole story: the largest customer sits there.</p>\n<p><em>Overlay revenue and risk.</em> The 12% customer is $X of revenue; if the need is a blocker, the expected loss from refusing (probability of leaving times revenue) may exceed the value of the next item, as in the break-even arithmetic in Worked example 5. I'd talk to them to learn the probability and the minimum viable set.</p>\n<p><em>Decision.</em> Deliver the minimum compliance subset of admin controls first if it is a true blocker (perhaps 2.5 person-months), then integrations, then performance in stages, giving each customer a dated answer. I'd avoid a custom build for any one customer and prefer general features. I'd also address concentration: three customers are 27% of revenue, which is a business risk to reduce by serving the broader base.</p>\n<p><em>Communicate.</em> Share the criteria and the order with all three, with what they get and when, and what the alternative is until then. What would change my mind: learning that the 9% customer's performance issue is causing active evaluation of a competitor.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Microsoft is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that enterprise-prioritization prompts at Microsoft separate buyer and user needs <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Microsoft data or product behavior is claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you separate a blocker from a preference? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By asking what happens if they do not get it. A compliance requirement without which the customer cannot legally use the product is a blocker with a date, and I would prioritize it by the revenue and risk attached. A preference, such as faster performance, is a request to compare on breadth. I would ask each customer for the consequence in their words and verify it: a security review that will fail without admin controls is a blocker; an admin console wish is a preference. Blockers get scheduled by deadline; preferences get ranked with the rest of the roadmap.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you protect against concentration and precedent? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;With a rule. Three customers at 12%, 9% and 6% make 27% of revenue, and ranking by their size makes the product theirs. I would ask whether the need is shared by other customers, using a quick survey of the other 197 and the support data. Work that serves several customers goes up; work for one goes through a paid or configurable route. I would tell each customer the decision and the reason, and I would avoid promising a date before engineering confirms scope, because a promise to one large account becomes a precedent for all.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c15-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C15-05 · Hard · execution · EX · Meta</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Two teams want the same engineers for the next quarter: the growth team, to run experiments to raise new-user engagement, and the integrity team, to reduce harmful content. How do you decide?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Whichever has the bigger numbers.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Sees that the goals are not in one unit, defines criteria that apply to both, and proposes an allocation.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates constraints (a minimum level of safety) from tradeable goals, uses guardrails and thresholds, sets a review date, and handles the politics with a decision rule and an escalation path.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I know the product only from public information. The first thing I'd notice is that the two goals are not in the same unit: growth is measured in engagement, integrity in the prevalence of harmful content. Trading them with one score would be false precision. So I'd separate the question into what is a constraint and what is a choice.</p>\n<p><em>Constraints first.</em> Some integrity work is not tradeable: legal requirements, commitments to regulators, and a minimum safety standard the company has set. I'd ask the integrity team which of their items are in that category. Those are funded first, as constraints, and the growth team cannot trade against them.</p>\n<p><em>What remains is a choice.</em> For the rest, I'd define criteria that apply to both: contribution to the company's goals for the period, size of the expected effect with a range, confidence, cost of delay (what gets worse if we wait), and risk (including reputational and user harm). Cost of delay is important for integrity: a harm that grows each week is different from an experiment that can wait.</p>\n<p><em>Sizing (invented).</em> Say there are 6 engineers. Growth proposes experiments with an expected +0.8% in new-user engagement, a 40% chance each of a positive result. Integrity proposes work to reduce harmful content views from 0.12% to 0.08% of views, a one-third reduction, with higher confidence. These cannot be compared in units, but each can be checked against a threshold the company has set (for example, a target prevalence).</p>\n<p><em>Allocation.</em> Not all-or-nothing. A sensible split: fund integrity to the level that meets the constraint and the target, say 3 engineers, and give 3 to growth, with a guardrail on the growth work (no experiment ships that raises harm metrics beyond a set margin). If the integrity target is not met at 3, move more. Review at the midpoint of the quarter with data.</p>\n<p><em>Decision rule and escalation.</em> If the two leads cannot agree, decide by a rule agreed in advance: constraints first, then expected contribution to company goals weighted by cost of delay. If they still disagree, escalate to the shared leader with a one-page tradeoff table, not a debate.</p>\n<p><em>Why this is not a compromise by default.</em> A 50-50 split has no logic; the split follows from the constraint and the target. What would change my mind: new evidence that the harm is growing fast, which would move engineers to integrity, or a growth opportunity that expires.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Meta is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that prioritization prompts at Meta can set growth and integrity goals against each other <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Meta data or internal practice is claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What is a constraint and what is a choice here? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Some integrity work is not tradeable: legal obligations, a harm that is severe and rising, and a minimum level of safety agreed with leadership. That sets a floor of engineers who go to the integrity team first. Above the floor, the allocation is a choice, and growth and integrity compete on judgment, not on one score. I would define the floor in advance, in writing, in terms of prevalence thresholds, not engineer counts, and let growth have the rest. Treating integrity as a constraint avoids the false precision of trading a harm reduction against an engagement number.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you handle the politics and the review date? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;With a decision rule and an escalation path. The rule: integrity gets the engineers needed to keep prevalence below the agreed threshold; growth gets the remainder, with a stated expected return. Review the split at mid-quarter against the prevalence trend and the growth results, and change it if either moves. If the two leaders disagree, escalate to the person who owns both goals with a one-page note that states the options and the cost of each. A visible rule and date reduce the sense that one team won.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c15-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C15-06 · Hard · execution · EX</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Option A is a sure, modest win. Option B is a big win that might not work. You have capacity for one. How do you choose?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;B has the bigger upside, so B,&quot; or &quot;A is safe, so A.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Compares expected values per unit of effort and considers risk appetite.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes expected value net of cost, shows how a cheap test changes the comparison, treats the probabilities as assumptions, and sequences the options.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd compare expected value net of cost, then see whether a test changes the picture. Suppose (invented) A is a $2,000,000 annual benefit with a 60% chance of working, costing 4 person-months at $25,000 each ($100,000). B is a $5,000,000 benefit with a 20% chance, costing 10 person-months ($250,000).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A: 0.6 × $2,000,000 = $1,200,000, less $100,000, is $1,100,000.</li>\n<li>B: 0.2 × $5,000,000 = $1,000,000, less $250,000, is $750,000.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On expected value, A is ahead even though B's prize is bigger. Per person-month, A returns about $300,000 and B about $100,000.</p>\n<p><em>Does a test change B?</em> The 20% chance is an assumption. Suppose a concierge or prototype test costs 1 person-month ($25,000) and reliably tells us whether B is viable. If we build B only when the test says yes (20% of the time), the expected value is 0.2 × $5,000,000 less 0.2 × $250,000 for the build, less $25,000 for the test: $1,000,000 − $50,000 − $25,000 = $925,000. The test improves B from $750,000 to $925,000 by avoiding the 80% of builds that would fail. B-with-test is still behind A's $1,100,000.</p>\n<p><em>Decision.</em> Do A first. Run the cheap test of B in parallel if there is any spare capacity, because the test is worth more than it costs. The choice depends on the probabilities, so I'd state them as assumptions and invite challenge: for B to beat A, its chance of working would need to be above about 27% ($1,100,000 + $250,000 = $1,350,000, divided by $5,000,000 is 27%, ignoring the test).</p>\n<p><em>Beyond the numbers.</em> Risk appetite matters. If the company needs a big bet (a strategic gap, a competitor), B's upside may matter more than expected value, and a portfolio view (several A's and a B) is better than choosing one. And the learning from testing B has option value. What would change my mind: evidence that A's 60% is overstated, or that B's market window is closing.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How does a cheap test change the comparison? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;It lets you buy information before paying the full cost. Option B has net expected value of 0.2 × $5,000,000 − $250,000 = $750,000, against $1,100,000 for A. Suppose one person-month ($25,000) of testing would show whether B works. If B works (20% of the time), you spend the full $250,000 and gain $5,000,000; if not, you stop. The expected value of test-then-build is 0.2 × ($5,000,000 − $250,000) − $25,000 = $925,000, much better than building B blind. It is still below A, but the gap has narrowed, and a cheaper test would close it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you treat the probabilities, and how would you sequence the options? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;As assumptions I would challenge: where did 60% and 20% come from? If they come from past launches of similar size, they are evidence; if they come from the team's optimism, I would discount them. I would run the sensitivity: at what probability does B beat A? Here B needs about 26% (so that 0.26 × 5,000,000 − 250,000 = $1,050,000). Sequence: build A now, use a small slice of capacity to test B's riskiest assumption, and decide on B next quarter with evidence.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c15-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C15-07 · Hard · execution · EX</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A senior executive wants you to build their idea next. The data suggests another item is better. How do you handle it?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Do what the executive says,&quot; or &quot;refuse and cite the data.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Treats it as a decision with evidence, brings options and a recommendation, and uses a respectful script.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Seeks the need behind the idea, separates what the data shows from what it cannot, proposes a cheap test, offers a way to include the idea, and keeps the relationship and the decision rights clear.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd begin by assuming the executive knows something I don't: a customer conversation, a strategic bet, a competitor's move. So the first step is to ask about the need behind the idea, not to defend my list. &quot;Help me understand what you're seeing that makes this the priority.&quot; Often the idea is a solution to a problem that can be solved in more than one way.</p>\n<p><em>Show the tradeoff, not just my view.</em> I'd bring the options with their costs. Say the executive's idea is X and my data points to Y. A one-page table: expected impact on the agreed goal with a range, effort, confidence, and what each displaces. &quot;If we build X, we defer Y, which we estimate at $A a year; X's value depends on assumption Z, which we have not tested.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Be honest about the data's limits.</em> Data on past behavior does not capture a new strategic bet. If the executive's case is strategic, my numbers on near-term impact are not the whole argument. I'd say what the data does and does not show.</p>\n<p><em>Offer routes that respect the executive.</em> (1) A cheap test of X's riskiest assumption in two weeks, with success and kill criteria agreed beforehand; (2) a smaller version of X that fits alongside Y; (3) a sequence, Y now and X next quarter with a date. These let the executive see that their idea was taken seriously.</p>\n<p><em>Use the script (FW-C15-07).</em> &quot;I see the need: [need]. To build X now we'd delay Y, which means [consequence]. What I can do is [test or smaller version]. If the test shows [result], I'd move X up.&quot; And keep the decision rights clear: if they decide to proceed after seeing the tradeoff, I'd do it well and record the assumption so we learn from the outcome, without saying &quot;I told you so.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>What I'd avoid.</em> Hiding the tradeoff to avoid conflict, quietly deprioritizing, or turning it into a contest of status. What would change my mind: the executive revealing a fact (a commitment, a customer deadline) that changes the value of X.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you show the tradeoff and not just your view? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;With a shared one-page comparison: the executive's idea and my top item, each with the goal it serves, reach, impact estimate, confidence, cost and what we would learn. I would ask them to check the inputs and tell me where I am wrong. Often the difference is in a single assumption, and naming it makes the conversation about evidence. I would present it as the decision they own, with my recommendation, and avoid framing it as the data against their idea.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What cheap test or compromise would you propose? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A time-boxed test of the executive's idea, such as a prototype or a manual version for a small group, with a threshold agreed in advance, while my top item proceeds. If the test passes, the idea moves up with evidence; if not, the executive sees it. Another option is a smaller version of the idea that fits beside the main item. I would keep the relationship by taking their need seriously and by being clear who decides: if they decide to proceed regardless, I would commit, record the risk and the signal that would reopen it.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c15-08\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C15-08 · Intermediate · execution · EX</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A feature used by 4% of weekly users takes about one and a half engineers to maintain. Should you retire it?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, it's a small share,&quot; or &quot;no, someone uses it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Looks at who uses it, the cost, and the alternatives.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies the cost against the revenue at risk, finds the break-even churn, considers lighter options (maintenance mode, simplification, migration), plans communication and a reversible rollout, and sets a decision rule.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>Who uses it, and why.</em> 4% of 2 million weekly users is 80,000 people. Share of users says little by itself: what matters is who they are. Are they heavy, loyal or high-value users (suppose their retention is 92% against 70% overall)? Do they use it for a job nothing else does? Are they concentrated in a segment we care about?</p>\n<p><em>Cost against what is at risk (invented figures).</em> At about $15,000 a month per engineer, one and a half engineers cost $22,500 a month, plus the opportunity cost of what they would otherwise build. If these users pay about $4 a month and some leave when the feature goes, the revenue at risk is the number who churn times $4. The break-even is 22,500 ÷ (80,000 × 4) = about 7.0% of those users leaving. If 5% leave, retiring saves $22,500 and loses $16,000, a net gain of $6,500 a month; if 10% leave, it loses $32,000, a net loss of $9,500; at 20%, a net loss of $41,500. So the decision turns on the churn we would cause, which I can estimate rather than guess.</p>\n<p><em>Options between keep and kill.</em> (A) Keep as is. (B) Put it in maintenance mode: freeze features, fix only critical bugs, and cut the cost to perhaps half an engineer. (C) Simplify or fold its core into another feature. (D) Retire with a migration path, a long notice period and an export. (E) Retire outright.</p>\n<p><em>How I'd estimate churn.</em> Look at what these users do instead when the feature is unavailable (a holdout or a past outage), survey the heaviest users, and test a deprecation notice with a small group to see who leaves. Also check dependencies: other features, integrations and support load.</p>\n<p><em>Recommendation.</em> Start with B and measure; if the estimated churn from retirement is under about 5% and the freed capacity has a clear, better use, move to D with notice and migration. If the users are high-value and the churn estimate is above the break-even, keep it in maintenance mode and revisit when the cost or use changes. I'd decide with a rule agreed in advance and tell users plainly what is happening and why.</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Who uses it; cost against revenue at risk; a computed break-even; options between keep and kill; communication and reversibility.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the 4% are your most loyal, highest-retention users? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then the revenue at risk is the loyalty of the group that sustains the product, not just their monthly payment, so I'd weight their retention and referrals. I'd lean toward keeping the feature in maintenance mode, learn what job it does for them and whether another feature could serve it, and only retire it with a replacement or a very clear migration. A small share of users can be a large share of the product's value.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you communicate the retirement? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Early, plainly and with a path: say what is going away, when, why, and what to do instead, with an export of their data and a migration guide. I'd give a long notice period (months, not weeks for an important feature), reach users in product and by email, offer support for the heaviest users, and keep a feedback channel open. I'd track complaints and churn after the notice as the guardrail, and I'd be ready to extend the date if the migration path is not working.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c15-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Ten exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Figures are assumptions unless marked, and the arithmetic was computed.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c15-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C15-01 · Compute a RICE ranking</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Score and rank: W (reach 50,000, impact 1, confidence 80%, effort 2); X (20,000, 3, 60%, 3); Y (80,000, 0.5, 90%, 2); Z (10,000, 2, 100%, 1). Then say what would change the order.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A team is choosing two features.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C15-03 and the sensitivity check.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> W = 50,000 × 1 × 0.8 ÷ 2 = 20,000. X = 20,000 × 3 × 0.6 ÷ 3 = 12,000. Y = 80,000 × 0.5 × 0.9 ÷ 2 = 18,000. Z = 10,000 × 2 × 1 ÷ 1 = 20,000. Ranking: W and Z tie at 20,000, then Y at 18,000, then X at 12,000. Z's 100% confidence is suspicious; at 70% its score is 14,000 and it drops below Y. X's confidence at 100% would raise it to 20,000, a tie for first. The order of the top four depends on confidence estimates, so I'd find evidence for those before deciding.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Report the ranking as bands: top tier {W, Z, Y}, then X, and use the goal to break the tie.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Scores to the unit imply a precision that the inputs do not have.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c15-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C15-02 · Same list, two goals</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> The options are: a referral program, a faster checkout, an improved search and a loyalty program. Say how the ranking would differ under two goals: (a) grow new users; (b) increase repeat purchases by existing customers.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The company has to choose its goal for the quarter.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None; reason qualitatively and name the reach and impact that each goal implies.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Derive criteria from each goal (FW-C15-04).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Under (a), reach is people not yet users: the referral program ranks high (its mechanism is reaching new people); search and loyalty barely matter; checkout matters to new users only at their first purchase. Under (b), reach is existing customers: the loyalty program and search improvements rise (they bring people back and make repeat shopping easier), checkout rises (friction on repeat purchase), and the referral program drops. The same four options produce nearly opposite orders, which is why the goal comes before the scoring.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Choose the option that is second under both goals if the company must serve both, and say what it gives up.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A compromise pick can serve neither goal well.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c15-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C15-03 · A weighted scorecard and flip test</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Options A (impact 5, reach 3, confidence 4, ease 2), B (3, 5, 3, 4), C (4, 4, 2, 3), each scored 1 to 5. Weights 1: impact 40%, reach 30%, confidence 20%, ease 10%. Weights 2: 20%, 20%, 20%, 40%. Compute both rankings and state what is robust.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A flip test.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C15-04.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Weights 1: A = 0.4 × 5 + 0.3 × 3 + 0.2 × 4 + 0.1 × 2 = 2.0 + 0.9 + 0.8 + 0.2 = 3.9. B = 1.2 + 1.5 + 0.6 + 0.4 = 3.7. C = 1.6 + 1.2 + 0.4 + 0.3 = 3.5. Order A, B, C. Weights 2: A = 1.0 + 0.6 + 0.8 + 0.8 = 3.2. B = 0.6 + 1.0 + 0.6 + 1.6 = 3.8.</p>\n<p>C = 0.8 + 0.8 + 0.4 + 1.2 = 3.2. Order B first, A and C tied. So the top choice flips between A and B when ease matters more: the decision depends on how much the team values ease against impact. That is the tradeoff to discuss. B is the more robust second-or-first choice, since it is first or second in both.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Choose B if the team is unsure of its weights, as the choice that is never worse than second.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A robust option is rarely the best under any one weighting.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c15-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C15-04 · Expected value per person-month</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> P: $3,000,000 at 50%, 5 person-months. Q: $10,000,000 at 15%, 12 person-months. R: $1,000,000 at 90%, 1.5 person-months. Compute expected value and value per person-month, and rank.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Capacity of 6 person-months.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C15.5.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> P: EV $1,500,000, $300,000 per person-month. Q: EV $1,500,000, $125,000 per person-month. R: EV $900,000, $600,000 per person-month. By value per person-month: R, P, Q. With 6 person-months: R (1.5) and P (5) total 6.5, slightly over; R plus part of P does not work. So fund P (5) and have 1 left, or R (1.5) and 4.5 for something else. A sensible plan: R first (cheap, high return), then P if capacity can be stretched or the date moved. Q has the highest prize ($10,000,000) and the lowest return per unit; test its 15% before committing.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> If the company needs a large bet, include Q with a test first.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> The probabilities are assumptions; Q's chance would have to exceed 36% for Q to pass P and 72% to pass R, so the ranking holds if Q's chance doubles to 30%.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c15-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C15-05 · A shorter deadline</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Three engineers have 6 weeks for 9 stories (about 2 person-weeks each). The date moves to 3 weeks. Compute the capacity and the number of stories; then compute the effect of adding one engineer (25% productive in week 1, 50% in week 2, full in week 3; the three existing lose 20% of two weeks to onboarding).</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The sponsor suggests &quot;just add someone.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C15-05.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> At 3 weeks: 3 × 3 = 9 person-weeks, 4.5 stories. The new engineer delivers 0.25 + 0.5 + 1 = 1.75 person-weeks; onboarding costs 3 × 2 × 0.2 = 1.2; net gain 0.55, for 9.55 person-weeks, about 4.8 stories. Adding a person adds only about 0.3 of a story in this window (0.55 person-weeks), while communication paths rise from 3 to 6. So the lever is scope: choose the 4 or 5 stories that deliver the core, defer the rest to a follow-up, or move the date.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Assign the new engineer to a separable task that does not need onboarding in the core code, for example test tooling, and say it will help later, not now.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Adding people early in a long project can pay; late in a short one it rarely does.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c15-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C15-06 · Design an MVP test</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> An app wants to add paid expert coaching. The riskiest assumption is that users will pay for it. Design an MVP test with success and kill criteria, and compute the confidence intervals for three outcomes with 100 offers: 30, 22 and 15 purchases.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Engineering says it would take five months to build.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C15-06 and C14.5.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> A fake-door test: show a &quot;Book a coach, $30&quot; button to 100 eligible users; when they click, ask them to pay and book a time, and fulfill manually with a coach. Success: at least 30 of 100 buy (30%). Kill: 15 or fewer (15%). Intervals (95%): 30 of 100 is about 22% to 40%; 22 of 100 about 15% to 31%; 15 of 100 about 9% to 23%. So 30 clears the bar with an interval whose low end is above the kill bar; 22 straddles and calls for more data or a redesign of the offer; 15 is at the kill bar, with an upper end of 23%. Guardrails: refund requests and the coaches' time. Cost: two weeks and a few coaching sessions, against five months of build.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Test willingness to pay with a pre-order instead of a purchase, which is cheaper but weaker evidence.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A manual service does not test whether it can scale, so scalability is the next assumption to test if this passes.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c15-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C15-07 · Fix a MoSCoW list</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A release list has 14 items, all marked &quot;must.&quot; Capacity is 60% of the effort. Describe how to fix it.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A planning meeting.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> IQ-C15-02.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Redefine &quot;must&quot; as an item without which the release fails its goal or cannot ship by the date (legal, core flow). Apply the test to each item. Then compare the remaining total with capacity (60%) and ask what leaves. Move items to &quot;should,&quot; &quot;could&quot; and &quot;won't this time,&quot; each with a reason, and date the &quot;won't&quot; items for review. Show options with costs where stakeholders disagree. The outcome should be a list whose &quot;musts&quot; fit capacity, with a buffer, and a written reason for each deferral.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Ask each stakeholder to rank their own top three, then compare across stakeholders, since people will give up their fourth item more readily than their first.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A strict &quot;must&quot; reduces conflict at the meeting and requires follow-up communication.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c15-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C15-08 · Write the no</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Write a four-part reply (FW-C15-07) to each: (a) a salesperson wants a custom report for a prospect before the quarter ends; (b) a designer wants three more weeks to polish a feature; (c) an executive wants a feature added to this release.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Each request is reasonable and does not fit the plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The script: need, tradeoff, alternative, trigger.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) &quot;I see the deal depends on seeing the data. A custom build would delay the reporting upgrade other customers wait for. What I can do is a manual export for this prospect this week, and a general version next quarter. If more than two prospects ask, I'll move it up.&quot; (b) &quot;I see the polish matters for the first impression. Three more weeks would push the launch past the event.</p>\n<p>I'd propose polishing the first-run screens and the payment path now and scheduling the rest for the next release with a date. If testing shows the rough areas hurt completion, we'll move them earlier.&quot; (c) &quot;I see why it matters. Adding it means dropping X or moving the date by two weeks. Which do you prefer? A smaller version could ship this release, and the full version in the next. If engineering estimates the full version at under a week, we include it.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Attach the cost of each option to the reply so the person can choose.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Offering alternatives takes more effort than refusing, and it preserves relationships.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c15-09\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C15-09 · Break-even effort</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Feature F has reach 40,000, impact 1, confidence 80%, effort 2 person-months. A competing feature scores 12,000. At what effort does F tie, and what do you conclude?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A sensitivity check on effort.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Four minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C15-08.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> F's score is 40,000 × 1 × 0.8 ÷ 2 = 16,000. It ties 12,000 when effort E satisfies 40,000 × 0.8 ÷ E = 12,000, so E = 32,000 ÷ 12,000 ≈ 2.67 person-months. F stays ahead unless effort is more than about a third higher than estimated. Since engineering estimates are often low, that margin is thin: I'd ask engineering for a range, check the riskiest part of the estimate, and treat the ranking as fragile.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Find the break-even for confidence: F ties at confidence 60% (40,000 × c ÷ 2 = 12,000 gives c = 0.6).</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Knowing the break-even tells you which number to spend time on.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c15-10\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C15-10 · A timed full answer</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Give a 20 to 25 minute answer to: &quot;You are the PM of a note-taking app. You can build one of three things this quarter: collaboration (share and edit with others), a web clipper, or offline mode. Which first?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Self-scored; record yourself. State assumptions aloud.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None. Invent only what you label.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Use the 25-minute budget in section 4, compressed.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The full spine (FW-C15-01), scored against the six behaviors in section 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> An outline of a strong answer. Ask for the goal; assume growth in weekly active users among students. Criteria: reach among the target users, impact on weekly activity, confidence, effort, strategic fit (collaboration creates a network effect). Size each with a chain and a range: offline reaches users with poor connectivity, which matters if the target includes them (C09); the clipper reaches users who gather research; collaboration reaches pairs and groups and spreads the product to non-users. Score, then change the least certain number (collaboration's effort is the largest and the most uncertain). Name the riskiest assumption (students will invite classmates) and a cheap test (a share-a-note link that does not need full collaboration). Decide, say what you will not do and what would change the order (a different goal, such as retention among existing users).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Pick the web clipper as a quick win and use the quarter to test collaboration demand, if the team is small.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> The biggest strategic bet carries the biggest uncertainty; a cheap test lets you learn before committing.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for four weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: goal and criteria.</em> Use IQ-C15-01, IQ-C15-02, EX-C15-02 and EX-C15-07. State the goal before any ranking, and derive criteria aloud.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: sizing and sensitivity.</em> Use EX-C15-01, EX-C15-03, EX-C15-04 and EX-C15-09. Do the arithmetic by hand, find the break-even, and say whether the ranking is robust.</li>\n<li><em>Week 3: tradeoffs and tests.</em> Use IQ-C15-03, IQ-C15-06, IQ-C15-08, EX-C15-05 and EX-C15-06. Name the fixed point and the lever, compute expected value, and design an MVP with criteria set first.</li>\n<li><em>Week 4: conflict and full rounds.</em> Use IQ-C15-04, IQ-C15-05, IQ-C15-07, EX-C15-08 and EX-C15-10. Practice the four-part no, a resource conflict, and two full 25-minute answers with a partner who changes the goal midway.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to say &quot;Why not do both?&quot;, &quot;Where did that number come from?&quot; and &quot;The VP wants the other one,&quot; at random. Practice returning to the goal in one sentence.</p>\n<p><strong>Keep a decisions log.</strong> For each drill, write the goal, the top choice, what flips it and what you deferred. After eight drills, check whether you always named the opportunity cost.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I state the goal and constraints before ranking?</li>\n<li>Did I derive criteria from the goal and say how I weighted them?</li>\n<li>Did I size options with a chain and a range?</li>\n<li>Did I run a sensitivity check and name what flips the ranking?</li>\n<li>Did I name the opportunity cost and what we are not doing?</li>\n<li>Did I identify the riskiest assumption and a cheap test with criteria set first?</li>\n<li>Did I handle the tradeoff among scope, time, quality and resources?</li>\n<li>Did I say no with a reason, an alternative and a trigger?</li>\n<li>Did I decide, and say what would change my mind?</li>\n<li>Did I finish with the two-sentence close from section 4?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> For each drill record the date, the prompt, the goal, the criteria, the decision, the sensitivity result, your score out of ten and the step you rushed.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Apply the chapter on real work over the next three weeks. Each deliverable doubles as an interview story.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> Take your team's current backlog or roadmap. Write the goal it serves in measurable terms and the constraints. For the top five items, write the criteria they should meet for that goal, and mark which items would not survive.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Rescore the top five with a simple scorecard derived from the goal (FW-C15-04), with ranges. Run the flip test (change the weights) and note where the order changes. Share the result with your manager and the lead engineer, and ask what is missing.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Find one item whose value depends on an untested assumption. Write the assumption map (FW-C15-06) and the cheapest test with success and kill criteria, before anyone builds.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Pick one request you plan to decline or defer, and write the four-part reply (FW-C15-07). Deliver it in person, and note the reaction and what you learned about the need.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Compute the opportunity cost of one planned item: what the same capacity could build instead and its estimated value.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 3.</strong> Write a one-page &quot;not now&quot; list for the quarter, with a reason and a revisit date for each item. Share it with stakeholders before the quarter starts.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> the goal-derived scorecard with a flip test (a time you made a priority decision defensible), the assumption map and test (a time you avoided building the wrong thing), and the not-now list with reasons (a time you said no well). Keep the numbers real, and keep what you did separate from what the team did.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c15-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Prioritization is a decision about a goal under limited capacity: state the goal and constraints, derive criteria from it, size the options with ranges, and name what you give up (the opportunity cost).</li>\n<li>Use frameworks such as RICE to organize the numbers, not to make the decision; define reach and impact against the goal, and always run a sensitivity check to see what flips the ranking.</li>\n<li>Tradeoffs are among scope, time, quality and resources; name what is fixed, pull the lever with the least cost (usually scope), and do not assume people or quality substitute for time.</li>\n<li>Find the riskiest assumption and test it cheaply with a minimum viable product, with success and kill criteria written first.</li>\n<li>Say no with the need, the tradeoff, an alternative and a trigger to revisit, and treat what you stop doing as a decision as important as what you start.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c15-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Marty Cagan, <em>Inspired</em> (2nd edition, 2017). On choosing and testing what to build, and on the role of discovery.</li>\n<li>Donald G. Reinertsen, <em>The Principles of Product Development Flow</em> (2009). Cost of delay and queueing <code>[V: Reinertsen, Celeritas Publishing, 2009 (WorldCat)]</code>.</li>\n<li>Eric Ries, <em>The Lean Startup</em> (2011). The minimum viable product and validated learning <code>[V: Crown Business, 13 September 2011, ISBN 9780307887894]</code>.</li>\n<li>Sean McBride, Intercom's description of the RICE scoring model (blog; dated 5 January 2018 on Intercom's site; accounts of when RICE was first used at Intercom range from 2015 to 2017) <code>[V: Intercom blog, &quot;RICE: Simple prioritization for product managers&quot; by Sean McBride; dated 5 January 2018 per the site and a content aggregator; the year RICE was first used is not established]</code>.</li>\n<li>Frederick P. Brooks, Jr., <em>The Mythical Man-Month</em> (anniversary edition, 1995). On adding people to late projects <code>[V: Brooks, The Mythical Man-Month, 1975 with later editions 1982 and 1995]</code>.</li>\n<li>Daniel Kahneman, <em>Thinking, Fast and Slow</em> (2011). The planning fallacy and the outside view.</li>\n<li>Annie Duke, <em>Thinking in Bets</em> (2018). Decisions under uncertainty <code>[V: Portfolio, 6 February 2018, ISBN 9780735216358]</code>.</li>\n<li>Bill Coughran, &quot;More wood behind fewer arrows,&quot; Official Google Blog (20 July 2011; googleblog.blogspot.com/2011/07/more-wood-behind-fewer-arrows.html), and Verne Kopytoff, &quot;Is this what a 'focused' Google looks like?&quot; Fortune (3 July 2012). The sources for CS-C15-02; Larry Page's earnings-call remarks of 14 July 2011 are known through press reports.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c16\"></span>\n<h2>C16 — Specs, Engineering and Design Partnership</h2>\n<span id=\"c16-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>&quot;Walk me through how you'd take a feature from idea to launch.&quot; &quot;Engineering says it will take three times longer than you expected. What do you do?&quot; &quot;Tell me about a disagreement with a designer.&quot; These questions look like behavior and process questions, and they are really tests of judgment about a PM's central job: turning a decision into shipped work with people who know things you do not. The interviewer wants to find out whether you can write down what a team should build and why, work with engineers and designers as partners, handle the friction that is normal in that partnership, and set and hold a quality bar.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code> from the round structures candidates commonly report: execution and &quot;working with others&quot; questions at Google, Meta, Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, Netflix, Stripe and others, often as behavioral questions (C31) that probe how you worked with engineering, and as scenario questions (&quot;the deadline is slipping&quot;) <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. Candidates for technical-leaning roles may get more depth on engineering partnership. On the job this is most of the week: specs, reviews, estimates, tradeoff conversations, launch checklists.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> Whether you are clear, collaborative and quality-minded <code>Inferred</code>. Interviewers listen for a spec that separates the problem from the solution and states what is out of scope; for estimates treated as ranges and negotiated through scope, not pressure; for respect for engineering and design expertise combined with a willingness to decide; for engagement with technical debt as an investment; for the other partners (legal, support, finance) involved early; and for a sensible quality bar and release decision.</p>\n<p>Six observable behaviors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You write or describe a spec that states the problem, goals, non-goals, requirements and metrics, and that someone could estimate and test.</li>\n<li>You treat engineering estimates as ranges with assumptions, and negotiate scope rather than the estimate.</li>\n<li>You bring engineers and designers into the problem early, and settle disagreements with evidence and clear decision rights.</li>\n<li>You discuss technical debt with its cost and a plan, as an investment.</li>\n<li>You involve legal, privacy, security, support and others at the right time.</li>\n<li>You set a quality bar, triage defects by severity, and make a go or no-go call.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Six patterns end execution answers: a spec that is a list of features, a spec that is a 20-page document no one reads, pressuring engineers for shorter estimates, treating design as decoration, dismissing technical debt as an engineering complaint, and involving legal or security after the build.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<span id=\"c16-c16-1-what-execution-is\"></span>\n<h4>C16.1 What execution is</h4>\n<p>Execution is turning a decision into a shipped outcome: the chosen problem and goal become a working change in users' hands, with the evidence to tell whether it worked. The PM's part is not to do the engineering or the design, but to make sure the team knows what outcome matters and why, the work is scoped and sequenced sensibly, obstacles are removed, decisions get made, quality holds, and everyone who needs to know does.</p>\n<p><strong>The split of ownership.</strong> The PM owns the <em>what and why</em>: the problem, the goal, the priorities, the success measure, and the decisions on scope. Engineering owns the <em>how</em> in the system: architecture, estimates, feasibility, and the quality of what is built. Design owns the <em>how</em> of the experience. Data science owns the rigor of measurement. Each has authority over their craft, and the PM has accountability for the outcome across them. This is why a PM's influence comes from clarity and trust more than from authority (C32).</p>\n<p><strong>When not to over-specify.</strong> For a small, reversible change in a trusted team, a few lines in a ticket and a conversation are enough. A heavy spec process for a button label is waste. Scale the documentation to the cost of being wrong (C02's decision classifier).</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> A PM writes a 20-page document with detailed screens and forty requirements, circulates it the night before kickoff, and treats it as final. Engineers find three requirements that conflict, designers find that the screens ignore an existing pattern, and nobody read the section on the goal. Two weeks in, the team is arguing about details and has lost sight of the problem. A PM who had written a two-page spec that opened with the problem, the goal and the non-goals, then met with the engineering lead and designer to shape the solution, would have had the same detail emerge as a shared product, not as a verdict.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Hidden risks.</strong> Two. <em>Confusing the document with the work:</em> the spec is a tool for alignment; a perfect spec that no one understands has failed. <em>Confusing authority with control:</em> telling people what to do produces compliance, not good work; the best specs leave room for the craft of the people who build.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-c16-2-the-prd-as-craft-problem-goals-non-goals-requirements-\"></span>\n<h4>C16.2 The PRD as craft: problem, goals, non-goals, requirements, metrics, risks</h4>\n<p>A <strong>PRD</strong> (product requirements document) is a written spec of the problem, goals, non-goals, requirements, metrics and risks that aligns a team on what to build. Teams call it other things (a spec, a one-pager, a brief, a press release and FAQ). Its job is alignment, and its length should match the stakes. FW-C16-01 is a skeleton.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Section</th>\n<th>What it holds</th>\n<th>A test</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Problem</td>\n<td>Who has what problem, with evidence; the cost of not solving it</td>\n<td>Can a newcomer say it in one sentence, with no solution in it?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Goals and metrics</td>\n<td>The outcome sought and how success will be measured, with a guardrail</td>\n<td>Is there a number and a threshold (C05)?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Non-goals</td>\n<td>What this project will <em>not</em> do</td>\n<td>Would a reasonable person have expected it to be in scope?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Users and stories</td>\n<td>Who is affected and the key scenarios</td>\n<td>Are the stories testable (C16.3)?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Requirements</td>\n<td>What must be true, in priority order (must, should, could)</td>\n<td>Can an engineer estimate each and QA test it?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Design and technical notes</td>\n<td>Constraints, links to designs, known approaches</td>\n<td>Does it constrain the solution only where needed?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risks, assumptions and open questions</td>\n<td>What could go wrong; what is unproven; what is undecided</td>\n<td>Is the riskiest assumption named, with a test (C15.6)?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Launch and measurement plan</td>\n<td>How it will roll out and how results will be read</td>\n<td>Who decides go or no-go, on what criteria (C17)?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A <strong>non-goal</strong> is something explicitly declared out of scope for a project. Non-goals are the most underused part of a spec and often the most useful: they prevent scope creep (<a href=\"#gl-scope-creep\">scope creep</a>, C15), settle arguments in advance and tell people what they need not worry about. A good non-goal is one that a reasonable stakeholder might have assumed was included: &quot;This release does not include editing from mobile web,&quot; not &quot;We will not rebuild the company.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Bad versus good (FW-C16-02).</strong> A <em>bad</em> problem statement: &quot;Users need a better search.&quot; It names a solution area and no problem. A <em>good</em> one: &quot;Shoppers who search for a product by name get no results on 18% of queries, and 60% of them leave without buying (invented); we want to cut zero-result searches to under 8% without reducing relevance.&quot; It states who, what happens, the cost and the target. A bad requirement: &quot;The system shall be fast.&quot; A good one: &quot;Search results load in under 1.5 seconds at the 95th percentile on a mid-range phone on a typical mobile connection.&quot; It can be estimated and tested.</p>\n<p><strong>Length and form.</strong> A feature of a week may need a page. A multi-team launch may need a longer document with a short summary on top. A common alternative is to write the future announcement first (&quot;working backwards,&quot; described publicly by Amazon <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon working-backwards material]</code>), then the frequently asked questions that would follow, and derive requirements from them.</p>\n<p><strong>A living document.</strong> The PRD changes as the team learns. Keep a decision log (what was decided, by whom, why) so people do not reopen settled questions, and mark what changed after review.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Writing forces clarity, and the written problem and non-goals prevent drift. <strong>When not to write one at length.</strong> When the team already shares the context and the change is small and reversible.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-c16-3-user-stories-requirements-and-outcomes\"></span>\n<h4>C16.3 User stories, requirements and outcomes</h4>\n<p>A <strong>user story</strong> is a short description of a capability from the user's point of view, usually in the form &quot;As a [user], I want [capability] so that [benefit].&quot; It keeps the purpose attached to the request. <strong>Acceptance criteria</strong> are the specific, testable conditions a story must meet to be considered done. A common format is <em>Given</em> a starting situation, <em>When</em> an action occurs, <em>Then</em> an observable result follows.</p>\n<p><strong>Example.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Story: As a shopper, I want to filter search results by size so that I only see items I can buy.</li>\n<li>Acceptance criteria: <em>Given</em> a results page with items in several sizes, <em>when</em> I choose size M, <em>then</em> only items available in M are shown and the count updates. <em>Given</em> no items in size M, <em>when</em> I choose it, <em>then</em> a message says so and offers to clear the filter. <em>Given</em> I have chosen size M, <em>when</em> I leave and return within a session, <em>then</em> my choice is kept.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>A good story (FW-C16-03).</strong> The acronym INVEST sums the properties: <em>Independent</em> (can be built without another), <em>Negotiable</em> (the how is open), <em>Valuable</em> (to a user), <em>Estimable</em> (engineers can size it), <em>Small</em> (fits in a sprint), <em>Testable</em> (has acceptance criteria). When a story is too large to estimate, split it by workflow step, by user type, by data case or by simple-then-complex, never by technical layer alone (a &quot;database story&quot; delivers nothing a user can test).</p>\n<p><strong>Outputs and outcomes.</strong> A story describes an output (a filter). The goal describes an outcome (more shoppers find and buy an item they can use). Tie each story to the outcome, and measure the outcome. A team can ship every story and not move the goal; the PM's job is to notice and change course.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Stories keep the user in view and acceptance criteria turn &quot;done&quot; into something checkable. <strong>When not to use them.</strong> For exploratory work or research, where the output is a learning and not a feature, state the question and the time-box instead.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-c16-4-working-with-engineering-estimates-pushback-tradeoffs-\"></span>\n<h4>C16.4 Working with engineering: estimates, pushback, tradeoffs, technical-debt conversations</h4>\n<p><strong>Estimates are ranges.</strong> Engineers estimate the effort of work with uncertainty, and the uncertainty is largest early. A common pattern is that early estimates are off by a factor of two or more in either direction, narrowing as the work is understood (the &quot;cone of uncertainty,&quot; first drawn by Barry Boehm in 1981 and named by Steve McConnell, who gives ranges of 0.25 to 4 times at the initial concept stage; Boehm presented the ranges as judgment, not measured data) <code>[V: Boehm, Software Engineering Economics, 1981; McConnell, Software Estimation, 2006 and 1998 naming; Bossavit, The Leprechauns of Software Engineering, on the origin as judgment]</code>. Ask for a range and its assumptions (&quot;5 to 12 weeks if the API exists; more if we need to build it&quot;), the riskiest part, and what would narrow the range. FW-C16-04 sets out the conversation.</p>\n<p><strong>Use the outside view.</strong> If past similar projects took 1.1 to 2.0 times their estimates, with an average of 1.45 (invented), an 8-week estimate suggests planning for about 12 weeks (8 × 1.45 = 11.6), within a range of 9 to 16 weeks, or cutting scope to meet 8. Do not pressure engineers to shrink the number: pressure produces optimistic estimates and hidden shortcuts. Negotiate <em>scope</em> (C15.4): &quot;If we can only afford 8 weeks, what is the best version that fits?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Handling pushback.</strong> When engineering says &quot;that is not feasible&quot; or &quot;that is too much,&quot; ask what makes it hard and what would make it easier. Often there is a cheaper route to the same goal, or a hidden constraint you need to know. Treat pushback as information. Your part is to state the goal clearly so that engineers can propose alternatives, and to decide when a tradeoff is yours to make.</p>\n<p><strong>Technical debt.</strong> <strong>Technical debt</strong> is the accumulated cost of earlier shortcuts or aging design choices in a system, which makes later changes slower or riskier. It behaves like financial debt (FW-C16-05): the <em>principal</em> is the work to fix it, the <em>interest</em> is the extra cost paid every time the team works around it. Example (invented): a team of 4 engineers in 2-week sprints has 8 person-weeks per sprint. Workarounds for a fragile module cost 15% of capacity, 1.2 person-weeks per sprint (the interest). Fixing it costs 6 person-weeks (the principal).</p>\n<p>Payback is 6 ÷ 1.2 = 5 sprints, 10 weeks. Over six months (13 two-week sprints) the fix saves 13 × 1.2 = 15.6 person-weeks and costs 6: a net gain of 9.6. If the interest is only 8%, 0.64 person-weeks per sprint, payback takes 9.4 sprints and the net over six months is +2.3: still positive but marginal. The fix pays within six months as long as the interest is above about 5.8% of capacity (6 ÷ 13 ÷ 8). Treat the conversation as an investment with a return, compared with feature work using the same sizing (C15.5), not as a tax or a plea.</p>\n<p><strong>Learn enough to ask good questions.</strong> You do not need to code. You need the vocabulary and the shape of the system (C21): what the main components are, where data lives, what is hard to change, how releases work. Attend design reviews and ask &quot;what is the riskiest part?&quot; and &quot;what would you cut if we had half the time?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Treating engineers as partners with ranges, assumptions and options yields better plans and earlier warnings. <strong>When not to defer.</strong> The PM still decides scope and priority; partnership does not mean avoiding decisions.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-c16-5-working-with-design-and-research-the-triangle-and-how-\"></span>\n<h4>C16.5 Working with design and research: the triangle and how it fights</h4>\n<p>Product, design and engineering form a triangle, and its sides pull against each other in predictable ways. FW-C16-06 maps them.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tension</th>\n<th>What each side wants</th>\n<th>A way through</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Product and design</td>\n<td>PM: ship the goal on time; Designer: get the experience right</td>\n<td>Agree the quality bar by layer (C10.8); test the contested design with five users</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Design and engineering</td>\n<td>Designer: a custom, polished interaction; Engineer: standard components, lower cost</td>\n<td>Compare user value, accessibility and maintenance; custom only where it matters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Product and engineering</td>\n<td>PM: more scope; Engineer: fewer risks and time</td>\n<td>Negotiate scope by the goal, with ranges (C15.4)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Roles.</strong> Designers explore options and own the quality of the experience; researchers produce evidence on users, ideally before the spec is final, not after; the PM frames the problem, brings evidence and criteria, and decides what ships. Bring designers into discovery, not only into drawing. Critique designs against the problem (&quot;does this help a first-time user find checkout?&quot;), not taste (C10.11).</p>\n<p><strong>When they fight.</strong> Disagreements are normal, and they are useful if they stay about the problem. First restate the shared goal. Then name the criteria each side is using (user value, cost, risk). Then seek evidence cheaply: a prototype test, a look at analytics, a technical spike. If the evidence does not settle it, the person with decision rights decides, explains, and the team commits (&quot;disagree and commit,&quot; described publicly by Amazon <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon's Leadership Principles, Have Backbone; Disagree and Commit]</code>), with a date to review if the decision proves wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Naming the tension and moving to evidence keeps the conflict productive. <strong>When not to escalate.</strong> Most disagreements can be settled at the team level; escalate when the issue is outside the team's authority or blocks the date.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-c16-6-working-with-data-science\"></span>\n<h4>C16.6 Working with data science</h4>\n<p>Data scientists and analysts turn questions into evidence. Work with them early: before you write the metrics section, not after. Agree the <em>definition</em> of success (the primary metric, guardrails and counter metrics, C05), what needs to be instrumented (<a href=\"#gl-instrumentation\">instrumentation</a>), and how it will be read. For tests, agree the unit, sample size, duration and decision rule before launch (C14). Give them the decision, not only the data request: &quot;We will ship if the lower bound of the lift is above 0.5 points and support contacts stay within 3%; can we measure that?&quot; Ask for baselines, ranges and what the analysis cannot show. Avoid asking them to &quot;find something in the data&quot; that supports a decision already made; ask them to attack your hypothesis. Build a habit of shared dashboards and of pre-launch reviews of what will be logged.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-c16-7-other-partners-legal-support-marketing-finance\"></span>\n<h4>C16.7 Other partners: legal, support, marketing, finance</h4>\n<p>Many projects depend on people outside the core team. Involving them at the right time avoids late blockers.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Partner</th>\n<th>What they need from you</th>\n<th>When to involve</th>\n<th>Cost of late involvement</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Legal and privacy</td>\n<td>A description of data collected, claims made, terms changed</td>\n<td>Before design is final; for anything touching personal data, payments, children or claims</td>\n<td>A launch blocked or redesigned</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security</td>\n<td>The architecture, data flows, third parties</td>\n<td>At design review</td>\n<td>High-risk findings found days before launch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Support and operations</td>\n<td>What is changing, how users will ask, known issues, scripts</td>\n<td>Before launch, with time to train</td>\n<td>A surge of tickets with no answers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Marketing and communications</td>\n<td>Positioning, dates, who the users are</td>\n<td>When the launch plan forms</td>\n<td>Messages that promise what the product does not do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Finance</td>\n<td>Pricing, cost of the feature, forecast</td>\n<td>When pricing or costs change</td>\n<td>A price or cost you cannot sustain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sales and customer success</td>\n<td>What is coming, for whom, what to say</td>\n<td>Before launch</td>\n<td>Promises that the product cannot keep</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Localization and accessibility</td>\n<td>Strings, designs, requirements</td>\n<td>During design (C09, C10)</td>\n<td>Retrofitting</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Each partner holds a constraint that is cheap to meet early and expensive late. <strong>When not to involve everyone.</strong> Match the list to the change; a copy change needs few of them.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-c16-8-delivery-rhythm-agile-practices-that-matter\"></span>\n<h4>C16.8 Delivery rhythm: agile practices that matter</h4>\n<p>A <strong>sprint</strong> is a fixed, short period (commonly one to two weeks) in which a team commits to a set of work and delivers it in a usable state. It is a time-box (<a href=\"#gl-time-box\">time-box</a>, C15). Agile practices are many, and a few matter most. FW-C16-07 lists them.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Short feedback loops.</strong> Ship small, show working software often, and learn from it. This is the point of the method.</li>\n<li><strong>A visible backlog in priority order.</strong> A <strong>backlog</strong> is the one list of work the team could do next, ranked by the PM against the goal (C15). <a href=\"#gl-backlog\">backlog</a> is refined before each sprint so the next items are ready (clear, estimated, testable).</li>\n<li><strong>Planning.</strong> The team chooses what fits its capacity and the sprint goal, not what a manager assigned.</li>\n<li><strong>Daily sync.</strong> Short; to surface blockers, not to report to a manager.</li>\n<li><strong>A demo or review</strong> of working software with stakeholders, to get feedback early.</li>\n<li><strong>A retrospective.</strong> The team looks at how it works and changes one thing.</li>\n<li><strong>A definition of done.</strong> The <strong>definition of done</strong> is the shared checklist a piece of work must meet before it counts as complete (for example, code reviewed, tests passing, accessibility checked, instrumented, documented). Without it, &quot;done&quot; means different things to different people and quality leaks out.</li>\n<li><strong>Limiting work in progress.</strong> Fewer things started at once means more things finished.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The PM's role in the ceremonies is to bring the goal and the priority, answer questions, and make decisions quickly, not to run the meetings or to assign tasks. Drop ceremony that does not help: a long meeting that produces no decision is waste. Teams that ship continuously may prefer a flow-based method with a limit on work in progress and no sprints; the principles are the same.</p>\n<p><strong>Scrum and Kanban.</strong> Two named methods are worth recognizing by name. <em>Scrum</em> is a framework in which a small team of one product owner, one scrum master and developers works in sprints of a month or less, with four events (sprint planning, the daily scrum, the sprint review and the sprint retrospective) and three artifacts (the product backlog, the sprint backlog and the increment) <code>[V: The Scrum Guide, Schwaber and Sutherland, November 2020]</code>. The product owner is the Scrum role closest to a PM's, since it is accountable for the value of the product and for ordering the backlog <code>Inferred</code> <code>[V: The Scrum Guide, 2020]</code>. <em>Kanban</em> is a flow-based method: work moves across a visible board, and a limit on work in progress caps how much is started at once <code>[V: Kanban Guide for Scrum Teams; university course notes on Scrum and Kanban]</code>. In one line, Scrum fixes the rhythm and the roles, and Kanban fixes the limit on work in progress and lets work flow continuously. Teams often mix the two.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Short loops catch wrong assumptions early and keep the team moving. <strong>When not to follow the process.</strong> When it substitutes for judgment; adapt to the team.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-c16-9-quality-bar-and-working-with-qa\"></span>\n<h4>C16.9 Quality bar and working with QA</h4>\n<p>Quality is built in, not inspected at the end. Set the bar before work starts (C10.8), write it into the definition of done, and test continuously. QA engineers and testers bring a different mindset (what could go wrong?) and know the system's weak points; treat them as partners from the spec, so that acceptance criteria are testable.</p>\n<p><strong>Severity and the release decision.</strong> Classify defects by severity: <em>S1</em> (blocks a core task, loses data, creates a security or safety risk, or breaks accessibility for a group), <em>S2</em> (a significant problem with a workaround, or on a main path), <em>S3</em> (minor or cosmetic). A common release rule: no open S1; S2 only with a known workaround and an owner; S3 backlogged. Make the go or no-go call with the facts: count by severity, risk of each, time to fix, and the cost of delay. Worked example 5 does this with numbers.</p>\n<p><strong>Other release criteria.</strong> Accessibility basics (C10.6), performance on target devices (C09), instrumentation working, rollback tested (C17), and support trained.</p>\n";
